  "answer_text": "The most widely accepted, \"official\" way to pronounce **doge** is **\"dohj\"** (or **\"dohzh\"**). \n\nTo say it correctly:\n* Say the word **\"dough\"** (like cookie dough).\n* Add a soft **\"j\"** sound (as in *jack* or *judge*) or a **\"zh\"** sound (like the *s* in *measure* or *treasure*) to the end.\n* Phonetically, it is written as **/do\u028ad\u0292/** or **/do\u028a\u0292/**.\n\n---\n\n### Why is this the \"official\" pronunciation?\n\nThe pronunciation has two main pillars of authority:\n\n#### 1. The Homestar Runner Origin\nThe deliberate misspelling of \"dog\" as \"D-O-G-E\" first gained internet fame in a June 2005 episode of the puppet-show web series *Homestar Runner* (titled \"Biz Cas Fri 1\"). In the video, the character Strong Bad refers to Homestar as his \"D-O-G-E,\" and Homestar pronounces it out loud as **\"dohj\"** (or **\"dohzh\"**). \n\n#### 2. The Creators of Dogecoin\nWhen Jackson Palmer and Billy Markus created the cryptocurrency Dogecoin in 2013, they adopted the Homestar Runner spelling. Both co-founders have explicitly confirmed that they pronounce it **\"dohj-coin\"**.\n\n#### 3. Dictionary and Historical Definition\n\"Doge\" was actually a word long before the internet. It refers to the historic chief magistrates of Venice and Genoa. If you look up this historical word in the Oxford English Dictionary or Merriam-Webster, it is pronounced exactly the same way: **\"dohj\"**.\n\n---\n\n### Common Alternatives (and why people say them)\n\nBecause \"doge\" was a text-based meme for years, several other pronunciations became popular:\n\n* **\"Dohg\"** (rhymes with *vogue* or *rogue*): Many people prefer this because it keeps the hard \"g\" from the word \"dog\" but uses the \"e\" at the end to make the \"o\" long. \n* **\"Dodge\"** (like the car brand or the game dodgeball): This is a very common visual misreading of the word.\n* **\"Doh-ghee\"** or **\"Doggy\"**: Some users playfully pronounce it this way to make it sound like a cutesy variation of \"doggy.\"",
